commits.message:
feat: support minimax-m3 style tool call structural tag (#713)

## Summary

This PR adds first-class MiniMax M3 structural-tag support, including
recursive XML tool arguments and exact matching between
runtime-generated opening and closing element names.

MiniMax M3 encodes JSON property names directly as XML element names:

```text
]<]minimax[>[<city>Singapore]<]minimax[>[</city>
```

For dynamic properties, the closing name must exactly repeat the
runtime-generated opening name. This equality cannot be expressed by
plain CFG productions alone.

## End-to-end example

Given the following tool:

```python
tools = [
    {
        "type": "function",
        "function": {
            "name": "create_order",
            "parameters": {
                "type": "object",
                "properties": {
                    "user_id": {"type": "integer"},
                    "shipping": {
                        "type": "object",
                        "properties": {
                            "city": {"type": "string"},
                            "zip": {"type": "string"},
                        },
                        "required": ["city", "zip"],
                        "additionalProperties": False,
                    },
                    "items": {
                        "type": "array",
                        "items": {
                            "type": "object",
                            "properties": {
                                "sku": {"type": "string"},
                                "qty": {"type": "integer"},
                            },
                            "required": ["sku", "qty"],
                            "additionalProperties": False,
                        },
                    },
                    "metadata": {
                        "type": "object",
                        "additionalProperties": {"type": "string"},
                    },
                },
                "required": ["user_id", "shipping", "items", "metadata"],
                "additionalProperties": False,
            },
        },
    }
]

structural_tag = xgr.get_model_structural_tag(
    "minimax_m3",
    tools=tools,
    tool_choice="required",
    reasoning_mode="auto",
)

compiled = xgr.GrammarCompiler(tokenizer_info).compile_structural_tag(
    structural_tag
)
matcher = xgr.GrammarMatcher(compiled)
```

The grammar accepts an output such as:

```text
<mm:think>Create the requested order.</mm:think>
]<]minimax[>[<tool_call>
]<]minimax[>[<invoke name="create_order">
]<]minimax[>[<user_id>42]<]minimax[>[</user_id>
]<]minimax[>[<shipping>
]<]minimax[>[<city>Singapore]<]minimax[>[</city>
]<]minimax[>[<zip>018956]<]minimax[>[</zip>
]<]minimax[>[</shipping>
]<]minimax[>[<items>
]<]minimax[>[<item>
]<]minimax[>[<sku>book-001]<]minimax[>[</sku>
]<]minimax[>[<qty>2]<]minimax[>[</qty>
]<]minimax[>[</item>
]<]minimax[>[<item>
]<]minimax[>[<sku>pen-007]<]minimax[>[</sku>
]<]minimax[>[<qty>5]<]minimax[>[</qty>
]<]minimax[>[</item>
]<]minimax[>[</items>
]<]minimax[>[<metadata>
]<]minimax[>[<source>mobile]<]minimax[>[</source>
]<]minimax[>[<campaign>may-launch]<]minimax[>[</campaign>
]<]minimax[>[</metadata>
]<]minimax[>[</invoke>
]<]minimax[>[</tool_call>
```

`user_id`, `shipping`, and `items` are fixed schema properties and
compile to ordinary literals.

`source` and `campaign` come from `metadata.additionalProperties`, so
their names are generated at runtime. The grammar captures each opening
name and requires the corresponding closing name to match byte-for-byte.
For example, this is rejected:

```text
]<]minimax[>[<campaign>may-launch]<]minimax[>[</source>
```

## Design

This PR introduces a serializable, grammar-local `DynamicTag(...)`
expression:

```text
open_prefix + capture(name_rule) + open_suffix
+ content_rule
+ close_prefix + backreference(captured_name) + close_suffix
```

Each occurrence is lowered into FSM edges:

```text
CaptureStart → name DFA → CaptureEnd → content → BackReference
```

Runtime name equality is therefore part of the grammar instead of being
enforced by a grammar-global MiniMax sidecar matcher.

This provides correct occurrence-local behavior for:

- choices/unions and concatenation;
- nested and sibling dynamic elements;
- recursive element content;
- token masks and tokens crossing structural boundaries;
- rollback, fork, reset, jump-forward, batch matching, and EOS;
- grammar optimization, compiler caching, and serialization.

Fixed property names remain ordinary literals and incur no dynamic-name
overhead.

## Supported MiniMax M3 behavior

The builtin `minimax_m3` structural tag supports:

- recursive objects and arrays;
- fixed and dynamic properties;
- `additionalProperties`, `patternProperties`, and `propertyNames`;
- strings, numbers, booleans, null, `const`, `enum`, and `$ref`;
- `reasoning_mode="enabled" | "disabled" | "auto"`;
- automatic, required, named, and disabled tool choices;
- one or multiple invocations where allowed.

The implementation reuses `XMLToolCallingConverter` with a recursive M3
dialect rather than introducing a separate schema converter.

Dynamic name rules must be byte-regular so they can be compiled into a
bounded DFA and intersected with delimiter-safety constraints. Infinite
regular languages such as `[a-z]+` and arbitrary `additionalProperties`
keys are supported. Unsupported or unsafe rules fail explicitly during
compilation.

## Performance and thread safety

Compiled grammars are immutable and may be shared across threads.
Captured names, accepted-byte history, rollback state, and mask scratch
storage remain local to each `GrammarMatcher`.

The closing-name mask fast path only checks the vocabulary range
matching the next required byte.

Using the local 200,054-token MiniMax tokenizer:

- plain-text accept: `28.83 ns/byte` fixed vs. `31.92 ns/byte` dynamic;
- closing-name mask: approximately `1.96–5.21 μs`;
- dynamic grammar compilation: approximately `50–57 ms`.

`ParserState` size is unchanged.

## Serialization and compatibility

Both `Grammar` and `CompiledGrammar` JSON serialization preserve
dynamic-tag semantics. EBNF print/parse also preserves the
`DynamicTag(...)` expression.

The serialization version is bumped to `v18`; older serialized grammars
must be rebuilt.

## Verification

- C++ tests: `101 passed`
- Full Python suite: `3290 passed, 678 skipped`
- MiniMax M3 focused Python tests: `45 passed`
- Focused ThreadSanitizer tests: `4 passed`, with no race reports
- pre-commit, Ruff, and `git diff --check`: passed

